        "year":  "2024-2025",
        "content":  "During 2024-25 North East Zone Cultural Centre (NEZCC), Dimapur conducted 151 programmes/events in which around 7228 artistes participated.\n\n1. \tHar Ghar Tiranga Festival- 2024\nThe Har Ghar Tiranga Outreach (Awareness Campaign / Musical Concert/Cultural Programme) festival was organised by the NEZCC under the Azadi Ka Amrit Mahotsav initiative of the Ministry of Culture, Government of India. The programme was held across three states of NE—Arunachal Pradesh, Assam and Nagaland—from 13th to 15th August 2024. Tiranga rallies, Tiranga Yatra, Patriotic dances, patriotic musical performances were conducted during the programmes. A total of 180 artistes participated, including school children, youth groups, NGOs and folk performers from the region.\n2. \tProgramme of Honourable Culture Minister Visits at NEZCC Shilpgram\nAs part of the Honourable Union Culture \u0026 Tourism Minister Shri Gajendra Singh Sekhawat Ji Visits and Review Meeting, a vibrant Cultural Programme was held on 1st September 2024 at NEZCC Shilpgram, Guwahati. The event featured traditional \u0026 tribal music \u0026 folk dances. A total of 96 artistes showcased the rich cultural tapestry of the region.\n\n3. \tFloral Tribute to Bhagwan Birsa Munda\nA Floral Tribute was paid at the statue of Bhagwan Birsa Munda on 15th November 2024 at the Parliament House Complex, New Delhi, in the august presence of the Honourable Vice President of India. Cultural presentations included Wangala Dance from Meghalaya and Chakhesang Tati Music from Nagaland represented NEZCC, Dimapur.\n\n4. \t Hornbill Festival–2024\nThe Hornbill Festival–2024 was held on 7th December 2024 at Kisama Heritage Village, Kohima, Nagaland. Folk \u0026 tribal dance troupes from all eight North Eastern states participated, including Tappu (Arunachal Pradesh), Bihu Nach (Assam), Lai Haraoba (Manipur), Wangala (Meghalaya), Cheraw (Mizoram), Singhi Chham (Sikkim), and Bizu Dance (Tripura). A total of 98 artistes showcased the vibrant cultural diversity of the North East during the festival.\n5. \tMaha Kumbh Mela 2025\n\nNEZCC, Dimapur participated in the Maha Kumbh Mela 2025 held at Kalagram, Prayagraj, Uttar Pradesh from 12th January to 26th February 2025. The Maha Kumbh Mela is considered the largest religious gathering in the world, attracting millions of pilgrims from across India and the globe. During the festival Cultural troupes from Arunachal Pradesh, Assam, Manipur, Meghalaya, Mizoram, Nagaland, Sikkim and Tripura presented traditional folk dances, Bhaona, musical dramas, and devotional performances. A total of 210 artistes showcased the tribal, folk \u0026 classical performing arts of the region with grandeur and devotion. In addition, 145 participants displayed and ethnic handicrafts, handloom textiles, traditional cuisines and souvenir items of NE. The festival served as a vibrant platform to highlight the rich cultural heritage of the North East on a national stage. The North East Pavilion received overwhelming public response and acclaim throughout the 46-day spiritual fair.\n\n6. \tShilpgram Mahotsav 2025\nNational Festival of NEZCC, Shilpgram Mahotsav 2025 was held from 17th to 21st January 2025 at NEZCC Shilpgram Complex, Guwahati. The festival featured cultural participation from 8 Northeastern States- Arunachal Pradesh, Assam, Manipur, Meghalaya, Mizoram, Nagaland, , Sikkim \u0026 Tripura along with troupes from other ZCC, -Telangana, Gujarat, Punjab, Haryana, Odisha, and Madhya Pradesh. It showcased a wide range of folk and classical performances including Bihu Nach, Lai Haraoba, Cheraw, Wangala, Dandiya, and Bhaona etc,. Ethnic cuisine stalls represented communities like,- Lotha Naga, Mising, Deori and Sikkimese. School performances and children’s cultural activities added youthful vibrancy to the event. Traditional music, dance, and devotional presentations like Borgeet and Sattriya were also featured. A total of over 700 artistes and participants contributed to this celebration of cultural heritage.\n7.     Musical Tribute to India’s Republic Day – ‘Har Kanth Main Bharat’ Programme\n\nMusical Tribute to India’s Republic Day – ‘Har Kanth Main Bharat’ Programme initiative by the Ministry of Culture, in collaboration with Prasar Bharati was held from 2nd to 16th February 2025 at All India Radio, Guwahati, featuring vocalists from Assam and Manipur. Artistes performed Borgeet and Hindustani classical music as a tribute to the Republic Day spirit. A total of 20 artistes participated in this nationally broadcasted musical celebration.\n8. \tOctave – North East Festival 2025\nThe Octave – North East Festival 2025, organised by SCZCC, Nagpur was held at Khairagarh, Chhattisgarh from 21st to 23rd March and at Solapur, Maharashtra from 26th to 28th March 2025.The festival represented all eight North Eastern states—Arunachal Pradesh, Assam, Manipur, Meghalaya, Mizoram, Nagaland, Sikkim, and Tripura.Traditional folk and classical performances such as Bihu, Sattriya, Pung Cholom, Wangala, Cheraw, Tamang Selo, and others were presented. Each participating state also showcased its handloom, handicraft, and ethnic delicacies, adding depth to the cultural exhibition. The event drew a large public response, with participation from more than 300 artistes and artisans across both venues. The Octave Festival served as a major cultural outreach platform, reinforcing the spirit of unity in diversity through North East India\u0027s heritage.\n9. \tBharatiya Kala Mahotsav–2024, Rashtrapati Nilayam\nThe Bharatiya Kala Mahotsav–2024 was organised by Rashtrapati Bhavan in collaboration with Ministry of Culture \u0026 Ministry of DoNER, Govt. of India from 28th September to 6th October 2024 at Rashtrapati Nilayam, Hyderabad, Telangana, inaugurated by Hon’ble President of India Smt. Draupadi Murmu Ji. This grand cultural celebration featured rich traditional performances, cuisines, and music from the eight north-eastern states of India—Arunachal Pradesh, Assam, Manipur, Meghalaya, Mizoram, Nagaland, Sikkim and Tripura. Folk/Tribal and classical dances, ethnic cuisine stalls, live bands, and choir ensembles highlighted the vibrant diversity of the region. A total of 425 artistes/artisans participated in this prestigious national festival."
