        "content":  "1st Edition North East Tribal Spring Festival\n(Uttar-Poorv Janjatiya Basant Utsav) from 19–23 April 2026\nThe North East Zone Cultural Centre (NEZCC), Ministry of Culture, Government of India, successfully organized the 1st Edition of the Uttar-Poorv Janjatiya Basant Utsav (North East Tribal Spring Festival) from 19th to 23rd April 2026 at NEZCC Shilpgram, Panjabari, Guwahati, Assam.\nThe five-day festival was envisioned as a vibrant platform to showcase the rich tribal heritage, folk traditions, visual arts, indigenous cuisine, handloom, handicrafts, and the diverse cultural expressions of the eight North Eastern States of India.\nCultural Participation Overview\nThe festival witnessed enthusiastic participation from across the region:\n●\tCultural Groups: 16 groups (220 artistes)\n●\tSolo/Folk Cultural Groups: 14 groups (197 artistes)\n●\tBand \u0026 Choir Groups: 8 groups (61 artistes)\n●\tVisual Artists: 22 artists\n●\tCuisine Stall Participants: 5 persons\n●\tHandloom \u0026 Handicrafts Artisans: 8 artisans\nTotal Participation: Approx. 513 participants\nCultural Performances\nA wide spectrum of traditional and folk art forms was presented, including Wangala, Nzanta, Hojagiri, Sarlamkai, Singhi Cham, Domahi Kikan, Rikhampada, Taapu, Keet Laam, Tenem, Gumrag, Ka Shad Mastieh, Cheraw, Bardwi Sikhla, Mamita, Bwisagu, Hamjar, Lengpum Chok, Borgeet, and Pepa Badan, among others.\nBand and choir performances from Nagaland, Meghalaya, Arunachal Pradesh, and Manipur added a contemporary and fusion dimension to the celebrations.\nVisual Arts Exhibition\nA major attraction of the festival was the Tribal Visual Arts Exhibition and Live Painting Camp, featuring artists from all eight North Eastern States. The segment showcased indigenous motifs, folklore-inspired creations, and contemporary interpretations of tribal life.\nThis platform facilitated meaningful cultural exchange while highlighting traditional knowledge systems and indigenous aesthetics.\nHandloom, Handicrafts \u0026 Cuisine\nThe festival hosted a vibrant Handloom and Handicrafts Exhibition-cum-Sale, featuring traditional textiles, bamboo and cane crafts, ethnic ornaments, and handmade artefacts from across the region.\nEthnic cuisine stalls representing different tribal communities offered visitors a rich culinary experience, reflecting the diverse food traditions of the North East.\n\nBihu Nach Competition – Highlights\nA major highlight of the festival was the Bihu Nach Competition, held on 20th April 2026, which witnessed enthusiastic participation from leading Bihu troupes and performers from Assam.\nA. Bihu Husori Competition\n●\t1st Prize: Junbai Bihu Husori Dal, Guwahati\n●\t2nd Prize: Maligaon Bihuwa Sangha, Maligaon\n●\t3rd Prize: Sunali Asom Bihuwa Dal, Morigaon\n●\tJury Award: Dakshayini Bihu Husori Dal, Guwahati\nB. Xaru Bihuwati Competition\n●\t1st Prize: Mayangshri J. Barman\n●\t2nd Prize: Krishnangi Kalita\n●\t3rd Prize: Hiyasmita Rajbongshi\n●\tJury Award: Nrityangana Gogoi\n\nC. Special Bihu Performance Awards\nSpecial recognitions were presented in categories such as Dhulia, Pepua, Namoti (Male \u0026 Female), Nachoni (Male \u0026 Female), Tal Badak, Gogona Badak, Toka Badak, and Sutuli Badak, honouring excellence in traditional performance arts.\nHandicrafts \u0026 Cuisine Participation\nCuisine Stalls\n●\tLotha Naga- Nagaland Group – 3 participants\n●\tBhutia-Sikkim Group – 2 participants\n●\tMising tribal- Assam-  3 Groups\nHandloom \u0026 Handicrafts Artisans\nA total of 8 artisans participated, presenting traditional handloom and handicraft products including textiles, cane crafts, and ethnic handmade items.\nThe Uttar-Poorv Janjatiya Basant Utsav – 2026 successfully celebrated the rich cultural diversity and living heritage of North East India. The festival served as a significant platform for cultural preservation, promotion of indigenous traditions, livelihood support to artisans, and strengthening national integration under the vision of Ek Bharat Shreshtha Bharat.\nThe inaugural edition received overwhelming participation from artists, artisans, cultural practitioners, and visitors, making it a landmark cultural event of the region.\nThe festival was graced by the Hon’ble Governor of Nagaland \u0026 Chairman, NEZCC Shri Nanda Kishore Yadav, along with Padma Shri awardees and distinguished cultural personalities from the North East region.\n**************\n\nPhotographs link for the year 2025–2026. :\nhttps://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1_DBkpczh6wlohBFesquUbzUV8vCS_GdS?usp=drive_link\n\nVideo links for the year 2026–2027. :\nhttps://www.youtube.com/live/4SeSxMK2QaE?si=eTWNDn67pk4K69eE"
